WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Pydantic models.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.918
Data from users and from LLMs is messy.

3
00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:09.300
Pydantic gives it a shape, and tells you precisely where it breaks.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.367
Pydantic turns type annotations into runtime validation.

5
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:18.867
It even coerces sensibly, so the string five becomes the number five.

6
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.200
And when something's wrong, the error names exactly where.

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.700
You hand raw data to model validate.

8
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.133
If it fits the declared shape, you get a typed object.

9
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:33.300
If not, you get a validation error that points at the failing field.

10
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.667
The rule is to validate at the boundary.

11
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.300
When a request arrives.

12
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.933
When an LLM returns structured output.

13
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.482
And when an outside API answers.

14
00:00:43.482 --> 00:00:45.800
Never deep inside business logic.

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.567
Let's see it fail on purpose.

16
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:51.600
An observation has a magnitude and a band.

17
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.967
A report has an id, a hazard flag, and a list of observations.

18
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.591
The word yes gets coerced into true.

19
00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:05.433
But the word bright can't become a float, and the error says exactly where: observations, zero, magnitude.

20
00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:09.233
Here's a powerful pattern for structured outputs.

21
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:12.167
The LLM extracts data from free text.

22
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.500
We validate it.

23
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.333
If it fails, the error names the exact field.

24
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:21.167
We append that message to the conversation, and the model fixes it on the retry.

25
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:23.700
Here's where it pays off.

26
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.400
Your research agent extracts asteroid reports from free text bulletins.

27
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.333
About one response in twenty has a magnitude like approximately twenty two.

28
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:38.867
Validation catches it, the error goes back to the model, and the retry comes back clean.

29
00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:40.867
Let's recap.

30
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.400
Declare the shape once.

31
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.800
You get a typed object, or an error with an exact path.

32
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:47.933
Always validate at the boundary.

33
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:52.414
And feed validation errors back to the model as retry prompts.

34
00:01:52.414 --> 00:01:54.100
Next up: async endpoints.
